WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
The Purdue Model is a practical reference for understanding industrial architecture.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.620
It organizes functions from the physical process and basic control through supervisory and

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.765
site operations to enterprise systems.

00:00:10.765 --> 00:00:15.620
The industrial DMZ adds a controlled buffer for necessary exchange between OT and IT.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.700
Each level has different responsibilities and consequences.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.580
Lower levels interact directly with equipment and require careful attention to safety,

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.121
timing, and reliability.

00:00:24.121 --> 00:00:28.140
Supervisory and site systems provide operator visibility, engineering authority,

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.420
and coordination.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.340
Enterprise systems create business dependencies and potential pathways toward operations.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.040
Use the model to improve asset inventory, segmentation, access control,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.860
monitoring placement, remote-access design, and incident planning.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.420
Review data moving upward and commands, configurations,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.190
and administration moving downward.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:48.900
Make cross-level communication necessary, limited, observable, and owned.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.131
The model is not a perfect map or a substitute for engineering.

00:00:52.131 --> 00:00:55.940
Cloud platforms, industrial Internet of Things devices, vendors, remote sites,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.955
and converged architectures may cross traditional levels.

00:00:58.955 --> 00:01:00.910
Current diagrams, operational knowledge,

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.140
and environment-specific risk analysis remain essential.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.885
The strongest use of Purdue is shared reasoning.

00:01:05.885 --> 00:01:08.150
It helps security, engineering, operations,

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.160
and business teams discuss how systems depend on one another and where controls should

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.715
protect physical operations.

00:01:13.715 --> 00:01:16.070
Let the model guide questions and architecture decisions,

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:19.100
while letting evidence and operational judgment determine the answer.
